<h1>Lingo Operators Module - Full Reference for the Flutter App</h1>
<p class="meta">Route: <code>/module/operators?step=N</code> &nbsp;|&nbsp; Module 5 on the learner's path (<code>OPERATORS_LEVEL = 5</code>) &nbsp;|&nbsp; Branch: datatypes-module &nbsp;|&nbsp; Generated from the source code. Screen by screen: layout, content, interactions, effects, animations, timings, scoring, adaptive path, sounds, haptics, state and navigation, plus how to build each piece in Flutter.</p>
<p class="meta">Written for: the Flutter developers who build the Operators module in the Lingo app. It follows the same shape as <code>module-screens.doc</code> (the Command module) and points to <code>module-rive-animations.doc</code>, <code>hex-riv-implementation.md</code> and <code>flutter-rive-implementation.md</code> for the Rive pieces this module shares. Every size is in the 390 x 844 design frame (see 2.2).</p>

<h3>Contents</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Module overview and screen order</li>
  <li>Shared foundation (route, layout, shell, header, bottom row)</li>
  <li>The screen engine: anatomy, stages, geometry and behaviour of every screen</li>
  <li>Shared building blocks (headline, Hex with speech bubble, answer rows, tile cards, result panel, rewards)</li>
  <li>Cross-screen systems (scoring, adaptive path, streak strikes, battery, sounds, haptics, saved state)</li>
  <li>Interaction stages and scenes, one by one</li>
  <li>Screens 1 to 40, one by one</li>
  <li>Lesson complete screen</li>
  <li>Timing and constants cheat sheet, colour palette</li>
  <li>Assets and files used</li>
  <li>Building it in Flutter (mapping, data model, algorithms, checklist)</li>
  <li>Known notes and gaps found in the code</li>
</ol>

<!-- ===================================================== 1 -->
<h2 class="nobreak">1. Module overview and screen order</h2>
<p>The module is one Next.js page, <code>src/app/module/operators/page.tsx</code>. The query parameter <code>step</code> picks the screen (default <code>1</code>); <code>step=complete</code> is "Lesson complete!". Any other value returns a 404 (<code>isOperatorsStep</code>: a whole number from 1 to 40, or <code>complete</code>). The progress bar total is <code>OPERATORS_STEPS = 40</code>: the progress bar counts <b>screens</b>, not stages.</p>
<p>It follows the PDF "5. Operators" (40 spec screens, one step each). The design is the Sequence / Command modules' design: a headline, one short line from Hex in a speech bubble, a picture, and the answers as rows or tiles, with Hex's result panel when an answer is checked. The module has <b>no robot, no hint button, no review loop and no audio</b>. What it adds is an <b>adaptive path</b> (strong learners skip repeat screens, learners who struggle are sent back once to the screen that taught the idea, see 5.2).</p>
<p>Screens 1 to 39 are one component, <code>OperatorsScreen</code>, driven by a screen definition (headline + a list of <b>stages</b>). Screen 40 is <code>RecapScreen</code> (its own layout) and <code>complete</code> is the shared <code>LessonCompleteScreen</code>. A <b>stage</b> is what the learner does before moving on: a <b>quiz</b> (a question with answers, checked with Continue), an <b>interaction</b> (something to tap, build, share, order), or a <b>turn</b> (a look-only interaction where Hex introduces one card, then Continue moves on). Most screens have several stages; the stage changes without changing the URL or the progress bar.</p>

