<!-- ===================================================== 2 -->
<h2>2. Shared foundation</h2>

<h3>2.1 Route and layout (<code>src/app/module/layout.tsx</code>)</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>ModuleButtonWarmup:</b> starts loading the shared Rive Continue button as soon as the module opens, so the first screen's button is already drawn.</li>
  <li><b>RobotWarmup:</b> preloads <code>command.riv</code> (the robot arm). The Operators module never shows the robot, so a Flutter build skips it.</li>
  <li><b>HexRiveGate:</b> holds the screens back until <code>hex.riv</code> has downloaded and parsed (once, app wide), showing a loading view with progress. If the preload fails it does not block.</li>
  <li><b>Theme:</b> the layout does not force a theme. Every card, panel and text colour in this module has a dark variant (listed in section 9). Only the soft background wash is light only.</li>
  <li><b>OperatorsRoute:</b> <code>complete</code> shows <code>LessonCompleteScreen</code> (level 5, accuracy = <code>operatorsAccuracy</code>); step 40 shows <code>RecapScreen</code>; any other step shows <code>OperatorsScreen</code> with <code>key = step</code>, so every screen is mounted fresh (all stage, pick and hint state starts clean on each step).</li>
</ul>

<h3>2.2 The shell: LevelTwoShell (over LevelOneShell)</h3>
<p>Every screen sits inside <code>LevelTwoShell</code>, which wraps <code>LevelOneShell</code> and adds a full-frame stage for the children plus a scene slot (the picture behind). Here the scene is always <code>SoftBackdrop</code>.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr>
<tr><td>Design frame</td><td>Everything is laid out in Figma's 390 x 844 frame. The shell measures the screen before first paint and scales the frame as one piece: <code>scale = min(width/390, height/844, 1.5)</code>. The stage is stretched to fill what the frame's shape leaves. CSS variables on the stage: <code>--stage-scale</code>, <code>--stage-w</code>, <code>--stage-h</code> (stage size in frame px).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Answer column width</td><td><code>COLUMN_W = min(stageW - 50/scale, 380/scale)</code> in frame px: 340 on a 390 px phone (25 px screen margin each side), never wider than 380 screen px. Rows, tiles and question pills use this width, centred.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Top position</td><td><code>stageTop(figmaTop) = figmaTop - 37</code>: the Level 1 header sits 37 px higher than in Figma, so everything pinned to the top (title, Hex's row, the picture) is moved up by it; what is pinned to the bottom is not.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Safe areas</td><td>Top and bottom use the device's safe-area insets (notch, home bar).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Soft background (SoftBackdrop)</td><td>A vertical gradient: #EAF3FF at 0%, #F7FAFF at 38%, #FFFFFF at 100%, behind the whole stage. Light theme only; the dark theme shows the plain page background.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Out of energy</td><td>If the battery is empty the stage is inert (no taps) and the out-of-energy sheet comes up (2.5).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Back button</td><td>Goes to <code>operatorsPrevious(step)</code>: step 1 goes to <code>/level</code>; otherwise the screen before, skipping any screen that is skipped for this learner (5.2). It always leaves the stage, even from stage 3 of 6, and does not clear a pending detour.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Continue handling</td><td>If the screen has a checked answer (<code>answer</code> is set), leaving first ends any strike or loop effect (<code>endStrikes</code>), then runs the screen's own continue.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Voiceover</td><td>Not used in this module (no <code>voiceover</code> prop). Hex's bubble types its text at a steady pace (4.2).</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>2.3 Header (OnboardingHeader, 52 px)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Close cross (goes to <code>/level</code>), previous arrow, Rive progress bar (<code>progressBar.riv</code>, artboard <code>LESSON_ProgressBar</code>) showing <b>step / 40</b>, battery (<code>battery.riv</code>), and a mute toggle. Same as the Command module's header (module-screens 2.3).</li>
  <li>The progress bar reacts to every recorded answer with its <code>correct</code> / <code>wrong</code> trigger (see 5.3 for which stage events count). There is no hint button on any Operators screen, so the bar plays the plain strike clips, never the <code>...Check</code> twins.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2.4 Bottom row: the Continue button</h3>
<p>The row holds only the main button (the hint slot is never used here: <code>hintText</code> is not set on any stage). It is the Rive <code>AllButton</code> artboard (<code>ModuleButton_SM</code>, 360 x 73), green when enabled and grey when disabled; the label is set on the artboard text. The row is <code>min(stage width, 450) - 12</code> wide and as tall as the artboard's ratio gives (<code>73/360</code> of that width: about 76.7 px on a 390 px screen); the drawn button fills it, and its bottom edge stands 20 px above the screen bottom (<code>BUTTON_BOTTOM_GAP</code>).</p>
<table>
<tr><th>State</th><th>Label and behaviour</th></tr>
<tr><td>Quiz, nothing picked</td><td>"Continue", disabled (grey).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Quiz, an answer picked</td><td>"Continue" enabled: pressing it <b>checks</b> the answer (the quiz may set another label with <code>ctaLabel</code>; none of the Operators quizzes do).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Quiz, checked right</td><td>"Next" (a quiz can set <code>nextLabel</code>; none do): goes to the next stage, or leaves the screen from the last stage.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Quiz, checked wrong</td><td>"Try again": clears the pick, brings a fresh Hex and line back, same stage.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interaction, not done</td><td>"Continue", disabled, unless the stage has taken the button (<code>holdButton</code>, used by the turn stages once Hex has finished and the card is lit): then it is enabled and each press calls the stage's own handler.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interaction, done</td><td>"Next": goes to the next stage, or leaves the screen from the last stage (an interaction can set <code>nextLabel</code>; none do).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Press feel</td><td>The press holds 150 ms (<code>PRESS_HOLD_MS</code>), then the caller's handler runs at 330 ms (<code>CLICK_DELAY_MS</code>) so the press animation is visible before the screen changes. Click sound plays on press. A shine sweeps the button about every 5 s. While a strike plays, the button shows the electric glow (0.67 s) and takes no clicks (not drawn disabled).</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>2.5 Out of energy sheet</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Appears 600 ms after the battery reaches 0. Dark overlay (black 50%) fades in over 0.25 s; the sheet springs up (stiffness 320, damping 34), 28 px rounded top, max width 430 px. Choices: try infinite free, or recharge with gems; "No thanks" leaves to <code>/level</code>. Same as the Command module (module-screens 2.5).</li>
</ul>

<!-- ===================================================== 3 -->
<h2>3. The screen engine: anatomy, stages, geometry and behaviour</h2>
<p>Screens 1 to 39 all use <code>OperatorsScreen({ screen })</code>. A screen definition is <code>{ step, title, stages[] }</code>. A stage is one of two kinds (the "turn" stages are interactions that never finish):</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Kind</th><th>Fields</th></tr>
<tr><td>Quiz stage</td><td><code>line</code> (Hex's words), <code>question</code> (optional, a pill just above the answers), <code>visual(context)</code> (picture between Hex and the answers; context = <code>{ attempt, picked, checked, correct }</code>), <code>layout</code> (<code>rows</code> by default, or <code>grid</code> for tiles), <code>compact</code> (56 px rows: supported, never used), <code>options[]</code> (id, text, icon), <code>correctId</code>, <code>rightTitle</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>wrongTitle</code> (default "Not quite"), <code>wrong</code>, <code>hintText</code> (never set here), <code>keepLine</code> (Hex and his line stay after the check: only screen 4's last stage), <code>ctaLabel</code>, <code>nextLabel</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interaction stage</td><td><code>line</code> (Hex's words, optional), <code>render(api)</code> (the interaction), <code>finishPanel { title, text }</code> (the result panel shown when it is done, optional), <code>ctaLabel</code>, <code>nextLabel</code>. The <code>api</code> is <code>{ slip(), done(), finished, lineTyped, holdButton(press|null), next(), dismissHex() (a no-op here), hexSpoken }</code>.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>3.1 The state of a screen</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>State</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>stageIndex</code></td><td>Which stage is showing (starts 0). Changing stage resets the rest.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>selectedId</code>, <code>checked</code></td><td>The picked answer, and whether Continue has checked it.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>answer</code></td><td>The latest recorded answer <code>{ id, correct }</code>, handed to the shell so the progress bar plays its reaction (id increases each time so the same result can fire twice).</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>attempt</code></td><td>Counts tries of the same quiz; each try re-creates Hex and his bubble (a fresh Hex entrance, the line typed again) and replays the picture (the quiz's <code>visual</code> receives <code>attempt</code> so its animation restarts).</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>finished</code></td><td>An interaction stage has called <code>done()</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>lineIsTyped</code></td><td>Hex has finished typing the stage's line (reset on every stage change and every try). <code>lineTyped = !line || lineIsTyped</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>heldPress</code></td><td>The handler an interaction has taken the main button for (<code>holdButton</code>).</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>roomHeight</code>, <code>pictureHeight</code>, <code>fit</code></td><td>The picture's auto-fit (3.3).</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>3.2 What each press and event does</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Event</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
<tr><td>Pick an answer</td><td>Click sound, tap haptic, the card bounces and turns green-selected (4.4). Pick again to change it. Nothing is recorded yet.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Continue on a quiz (check)</td><td><code>checked = true</code>; <code>record(correct)</code>: the first answer to this question is noted for the score (5.1); if wrong, the screen is noted as struggled (<code>noteStruggle(step, "miss")</code>); answer haptic (correct 250 ms, wrong 80/40/80); the progress bar reaction; Hex's line leaves (unless <code>keepLine</code>); the result panel slides up; the answers rise.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Continue after a right quiz</td><td><code>advance()</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Continue after a wrong quiz ("Try again")</td><td><code>tryAgain()</code>: clear pick, clear checked, clear the bar's answer, <code>lineIsTyped = false</code>, <code>attempt += 1</code>. The right answer is <b>never shown</b> after a wrong check: only the picked card turns red (the engine passes an empty <code>correctId</code> to the cards while the check is wrong).</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>slip()</code> (an interaction)</td><td><code>record(false)</code>: same as a wrong check (first-try miss for this stage, struggle noted, wrong haptic, bar plays <code>wrong</code>, streak resets). It does not show a panel; the interaction shows its own note and shake.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>done()</code> (an interaction)</td><td>Ignored if already finished. <code>record(true)</code> (right haptic, bar plays <code>correct</code>, streak +1), <code>finished = true</code>. If the stage has a <code>finishPanel</code> the result panel (right look) slides up with confetti and "+10 XP"; Hex's line leaves.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Continue on an interaction</td><td>If <code>heldPress</code> is set and the stage is not finished: calls it (a turn stage's handler calls <code>next()</code>). Otherwise <code>advance()</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>advance()</code></td><td>From the last stage: go to <code>operatorsNext(step)</code> (a page change). Otherwise: <code>stageIndex += 1</code> and reset pick, checked, answer, hint, attempt, finished and <code>lineIsTyped</code>. There is no transition effect between stages other than the new stage's own entrance animations.</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="note"><b>Every interaction that calls <code>done()</code> counts as a right answer</b> for the progress bar and streak, even look-only ones such as the flip carousel (screen 1) or the step-by-step screens. Turn stages and stages that never call <code>done()</code> do not touch the bar.</div>

<h3>3.3 Geometry</h3>
<p>Everything between the headline and the Continue button is one absolute block. All numbers are frame px.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>What</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Headline</td><td>Absolute at stage top 79 (Figma 116), centred. One line: the block below starts at <b>125</b>; two lines: at <b>163</b> (<code>TITLE_END</code>).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Body block</td><td>Absolute, full width, <code>top = title end</code>, <code>bottom = bodyBottom</code>, a column, items centred, 8 px gap. Its bottom padding animates (500 ms ease-out) from 0 to <code>risen</code> once the stage is checked / finished ("gone"), so the room shrinks as the result panel arrives.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hex row</td><td>First child, full width, 16 px side padding, centred. Present only when the stage has a line. Collapses (height to 0, opacity to 0) over 0.35 s when the line leaves.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Room</td><td>The rest of the block (flex 1, min height 0): the picture or interaction is centred in it, both ways.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Picture auto-fit</td><td>The picture block (gap 8) is measured with the room. <code>fit = clamp(roomHeight / pictureHeight, 0.6, 1)</code> (<code>MIN_FIT = 0.6</code>); the picture is scaled by <code>fit</code> about its centre (it never grows, never shrinks below 60%). Re-measured whenever either size changes (resize observers).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Quiz answers</td><td>Pinned to the button row: bottom edge at <code>aboveButtons(28)</code> = 20 + button height + 28 (about 124.7 px up from the bottom on a 390 px phone). Left edge on the answer column.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>answersHeight</code></td><td>Rows: <code>n * 76 + (n - 1) * 6</code> (compact: 56 per row). Tiles: 2 answers = 184; 3 = 110; 4 = 104 * 2 + 14 = 222. (Tile count is the number of options clamped to 2 to 4.)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>bodyBottom</code></td><td>Quiz: <code>aboveButtons(28) + answersHeight + 10 + questionHeight + 8</code>. Interaction: <code>aboveButtons(24)</code>. <code>questionHeight = 0</code> without a question, else <code>ceil(question.length / 36) * 22 + 14</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>risen</code></td><td>Quiz rows: <b>150</b> (<code>LIST_RISE</code>); quiz tiles: <b>180</b> (<code>CARDS_RISE</code>); an interaction with a <code>finishPanel</code>: <b>215</b> (<code>PANEL_ROOM</code>); an interaction without one: 0.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Question pill</td><td>Absolute, on the answer column, bottom at <code>aboveButtons(28) + answersHeight + 10</code>; white 85% (dark: #15181E 85%), 12 px radius, padding 12 x 6, centred 16 px / 22 px, weight 600, #444444. It comes in with the answers (opacity 0 to 1 and rising from 90 px, see 4.4) and rises with them when checked (150 rows, 180 tiles) on the panel's spring.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interaction width</td><td>Interactions draw themselves in a 340 px column (some 316 px cards inside); a few (multi-pick) 354 px. The flip carousel is the full stage width.</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ===================================================== 4 -->
<h2>4. Shared building blocks</h2>

<h3>4.1 Headline (ScreenTitle, <code>parseTitle</code>)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Poppins 600, line-height 134%, #444444 (dark: white), centred, placed at Figma top 116 (stage top 79). Size from the length: <code>parts = title.split("*")</code> (every odd part is teal); <code>length</code> = letters without the asterisks; <code>twoLines = length &gt; 25</code>; <code>size = length &lt;= 20 ? 28 : 24</code> px. The per-screen result is in the table in section 1.</li>
  <li>One line: never wraps (<code>max-content</code> width, stays centred even if a little wider than the column). Two lines: width = the 340 px column; wraps where the text falls.</li>
  <li>Teal words: the app's gradient, <code>linear-gradient(90deg, #00B8A9 0%, #1387B3 33.17%)</code>, clipped to the text.</li>
  <li>Animation: fades from opacity 0 and slides in from 24 px above, spring (stiffness 140, damping 16), delay 0.15 s. It is keyed by the screen number, so it plays once per screen, not per stage.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4.2 Hex with speech bubble (HexBubble)</h3>
<p>Same component and rig as the Command module (module-screens 3.2, module-rive-animations section 5). What this module sets:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Part</th><th>Details</th></tr>
<tr><td>Hex</td><td>Rive character from <code>hex.riv</code>, played the "onboarding" way, in a 110 x 110 slot, always on the <b>left</b> of the bubble (<code>hex="left"</code>). Enters with the enter trigger; an ambient idle trigger fires every 4 s; he talks while a voiceover plays (none here). Each stage and each retry gets a new entry key <code>operators-{step}-{stageIndex}-{attempt}</code>, so Hex enters afresh. With <code>keepLine</code> the key does not change with the attempt.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bubble size</td><td><code>bubbleSize(line)</code>: width <b>190</b> if the line is 50 letters or fewer, <b>240</b> up to 90, else <b>262</b>. The bubble keeps what it likes but shrinks to the row: the row is the stage width less 16 px each side, minus Hex's 110, so about 248 at most on a 390 px phone (240 and 262 both come out near 248). The line wraps inside; the bubble is at least 86 px tall (padding 13 y, 12 x) and grows with the text. About 17 letters fit a line at 190 px, about 25 at 240 px (Poppins 16 px medium).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bubble look</td><td>White rounded (10 px) box with a soft blue drop edge <code>drop-shadow(0 3px 0 rgba(18,140,179,0.5))</code> (dark: #1A1C22 fill). Tail: a 15 px square rotated 45 degrees at the middle of the left side, pointing at Hex. Text 16 px, weight 500, line-height 1.24, #444444 (dark white), centred.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bubble enter</td><td>Opacity 0 to 1, scale 0.85 to 1, sliding 8 px out from the tail side. Spring (stiffness 260, damping 20), after a 350 ms delay so Hex has started moving.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typing effect</td><td>The line types letter by letter: 350 ms start delay, then 55 ms per letter. The whole line is laid out from the start (unseen letters invisible) so centred text never shifts. When the last letter appears the bubble calls <code>onTyped</code>: the screen sets <code>lineIsTyped</code> and interactions waiting for Hex (the flip carousel's demo, the turn stages' Continue) start.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Leaving</td><td>When a quiz is checked or an interaction is done (and not <code>keepLine</code>): the Hex row collapses (0.35 s) while the bubble bounces right by 18 px then flies off to the left (two window widths) in 0.15 s (easeOut then backIn); Hex follows 80 ms later, sliding off left in 0.2 s easeIn with the <code>exitLeft</code> trigger; during the exit Hex is drawn over the bubble so it passes behind him. The result panel then brings its own Hex.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>4.3 Answer rows (AnswerCards, list layout)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>2 to 3 white rows (never more here), each <b>76 px</b> tall, 6 px apart, 18 px radius, 1 px border (<code>black 4%</code>; dark #818185), shadow <code>1px 1px 20.9px -13px rgba(0,0,0,.25)</code>, padding 14 px each side, dark fill #15181E. Compact rows (56 px, no radio circle, 12 px gaps) exist in the engine but no Operators stage uses them.</li>
  <li>Inside, left to right: a picture box 46.68 x 47.31 (picture fitted inside), 22 px gap, the text (14 px / 22 px, weight 500, #444444, takes the rest; a long option wraps to two lines), and at the row's end a 34 px empty circle slot (a spacer: no radio is drawn; gap 34 px between the left group and the circle).</li>
  <li>Pictures: options made with <code>neutral(...)</code> take the Sequence icons in turn so a picture never hints at the right answer: <code>icon-order.svg</code>, <code>icon-actions.svg</code>, <code>icon-goal.svg</code>, <code>icon-short.svg</code> (then round again). Yes/No and True/False use <code>icon-goal.svg</code> and <code>icon-short.svg</code>. Where an option has its own picture (money, chocolate, chair) it is listed on its screen.</li>
  <li>Enter: the list fades and rises from 90 px below; opacity 0.3 s and y spring (stiffness 110, damping 13), both delayed 0.3 s. It is a new mount for each stage (keyed by the stage index), so each question's answers come in afresh.</li>
  <li>Selected: the row bounces (scale 1, 0.95, 1.05, 1 in 0.4 s) and turns selected: border #01A17F (the app's primary), fill #F2FBF8 (dark #0F2921). Press scales to 0.97.</li>
  <li>Checked right: same green look. Checked wrong: the picked row turns red (border #E5484D, fill #FFF1F1, dark #2A1517); no row turns green (see 3.2). A checked list takes no more taps.</li>
  <li>After a check the list rises by <b>150 px</b> on the result panel's spring (stiffness 360, damping 32), so the panel never covers it.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4.4 Tile cards (TileCards)</h3>
<p>Used when a quiz has <code>layout: "grid"</code>. The number of options (clamped 2 to 4) chooses the arrangement:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Options</th><th>Layout</th><th>Card height</th><th>Picture box</th><th>Text</th></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>pair: two big cards side by side, 14 px apart</td><td>184</td><td>95 x 83</td><td>16 px, line-height 1</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>tiles: three small tiles in a row (not used here)</td><td>110</td><td>full width x 46</td><td>16 px / 20 px</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>grid: 2 x 2, 14 px gaps</td><td>104</td><td>56 x 48</td><td>15 px / 20 px</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
  <li>Cards: 8 px radius, 4 px border (white at rest, transparent in dark), fill white (dark #15181E), shadow <code>1px 1px 6px 1px rgba(0,0,0,.15)</code>, picture over text centred, 6 px side padding, text weight 600 #444444. Same enter (0.3 s delay, rise from 90 px), the select bounce [1, 0.95, 1.05, 1], press scale 0.96, selected / right / wrong looks, and no-green-after-wrong rule as the rows. After a check the tiles rise by <b>180 px</b>.</li>
  <li>Quizzes with tiles: the 2 x 2 "which action / which operator" questions (screens 2 and 31), the Yes/No and True/False pairs (screens 3, 16, 19, 20, 30, 37), Calculate / Compare (6) and the &gt; / &lt; pair (26). Tile pictures follow the same neutral icons; the &gt; / &lt; tiles show the symbol as the text with <code>icon-order.svg</code> / <code>icon-actions.svg</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4.5 Result panel (FeedbackPanel, <code>centred</code>) and what feeds it</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Slides up from the bottom behind the Continue button (button row above the panel) with a spring (stiffness 360, damping 32). It stays mounted while closed so Hex's canvas is loaded. The panel is the Sequence module's "centred" sheet: the text column (190 px wide, 6 px gap) and Hex (drawn 109.65 x 79.95) form one centred group 24 px apart, 324 px wide, min height 94, padding top 15, bottom 124, 3 px border, 32 px top radius.</li>
  <li>Right look: fill #E3F6F3 (dark #0F2B28), border #7FD8CC (dark #1E5F59), title #01A17F (22 px / 28 px, semibold, never wraps), bold words #04ACAB. Wrong look: fill #FDECEC (dark #2E1517), border #D42421 (dark #6B2A2E), title #D42421, bold #E5484D. Body 14 px, weight 500, #444444 (dark white 80%), line-height 20 px.</li>
  <li><b>Content:</b> a quiz uses <code>rightTitle</code> + <code>right</code> after a right check, or <code>wrongTitle</code> (default "Not quite") + <code>wrong</code> after a wrong one. An interaction shows its <code>finishPanel</code> (right look) when done; an interaction with no <code>finishPanel</code> shows no panel at all. Panel titles are 1 to 2 words and texts at most two lines of the 190 px column (about 26 letters a line).</li>
  <li>Panel Hex: enters 250 ms after the sheet starts; on a right result he plays the thumbs-up cue (<code>continueThumb</code>) 900 ms after entering, held about 2.8 s before the ambient idle resumes. No cue on a wrong result.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4.6 Reward effects</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Confetti:</b> on a right result (a right quiz check, or an interaction done that has a <code>finishPanel</code>): a full-screen canvas burst of 120 square pieces, 6 to 14 px, 8 colours (#FFC107 #FF5722 #E91E63 #9C27B0 #3F51B5 #00BCD4 #4CAF50 #8BC34A), shot up from the bottom centre with random velocity (vx up to +/-6, vy -10 to -25), gravity 0.35 per frame, random spin. Plays once each time it turns on.</li>
  <li><b>XP burst:</b> a big "+10 XP" (56 px bold, #F5A623, up to 70% opacity) in the middle of the screen: rises from 60 px below with a bouncy spring scale (0.4 to 1, stiffness 300, damping 9), fades in, holds, then leaves 110 px upward, over 1.6 s (key times 0, 0.2, 0.65, 1; delay 0.15 s).</li>
  <li>The XP shown is decoration only: no XP total is kept by this module. Lesson complete shows a fixed +40.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4.7 The card look the scenes share</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>CARD:</b> 8 px radius, 4 px border white (dark transparent), shadow <code>1px 1px 6px 1px rgba(0,0,0,.15)</code>. Fills: LAVENDER #F6F4FE (dark #0F1F26), CREAM #FFF9F0 (dark #0F1F26), WHITE white (dark #15181E); good #E3F6F3 (dark #0F2B28); warn #FFF4E3 (dark #4A3418).</li>
  <li>Value block (a number in an expression): border 2 #E4DFF7, fill #F6F4FE, text #444444, edge shadow <code>0 3px 0 #D3CCF0</code> (dark: border #3B3670, fill #2E2A5C, white text, edge #1B1840). Operator block: fill #08A4AD, white text, edge <code>0 3px 0 #067F86</code>.</li>
  <li>An operator is any token made only of <code>+ - * / % &lt; &gt; = ! ( )</code> or the words <code>and</code>, <code>or</code>, <code>not</code> (regex <code>^([+\-*/%&lt;&gt;=!()]+|and|or|not)$</code>). Operators draw teal; numbers and names draw as plain values.</li>
</ul>

<!-- ===================================================== 5 -->
<h2>5. Cross-screen systems</h2>

<h3>5.1 Scoring (<code>operatorsScore.ts</code> over <code>moduleScore.ts</code>)</h3>
<p>The module keeps its own record of how each question went, in session storage under the prefix <code>lingo.operators</code>.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Item</th><th>Rule</th></tr>
<tr><td>Question id</td><td><code>questionId(step, stage) = step * 10 + stage</code> (stage index from 0). Screen 12's stage 1 is 121; screen 30's stage 7 is 307. (A screen can therefore have at most 10 scored stages.)</td></tr>
<tr><td>First try</td><td><code>noteAnswer(step, stage, correct)</code> stores <code>firstTry[id] = correct</code> the first time only; later answers to the same question never change it. A miss stays a miss. Stored as <code>lingo.operators-first-try</code> (a record id to boolean).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Struggle</td><td><code>noteStruggle(step, "miss" | "hint")</code>: a wrong attempt (a wrong quiz check, or an interaction <code>slip()</code>) marks the whole <b>screen</b> as struggled; a miss outranks a hint. Stored as <code>lingo.operators-assisted</code> (step to "miss" or "hint"). There is no hint button here, so only "miss" occurs.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>struggled(step)</code></td><td>True when the screen has a miss or hint on record.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>missedStage(step, stage)</code></td><td>True when that stage was answered and the first try was wrong.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>answeredClean(step)</code></td><td><code>firstTry[step*10 + 0] === true &amp;&amp; !struggled(step)</code>. Only <b>stage 0</b> of the screen is looked at, plus "no miss anywhere on the screen". A screen whose stage 0 never records (a turn stage) can never be clean.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accuracy</td><td><code>operatorsAccuracy()</code> = share (0 to 100, rounded) of the recorded questions answered right the first time; 100 if none was recorded. Shown on Lesson complete.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rewards record</td><td><code>lingo.operators-rewards</code> exists (one claim per run) but this module claims nothing.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reset</td><td><code>resetOperatorsScore()</code> clears all three keys when the module is started from the path. Reloading a screen keeps them (session storage); a new browser session starts clean.</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>What records.</b> A quiz stage records when it is checked. An interaction stage records on its first <code>slip()</code> (a miss) or on <code>done()</code> (a right), whichever is first. So a stage that is slipped and then finished is a miss; a stage finished without a slip is right. The stages that never slip and always finish right (flip carousel, step-by-step, share, fill rows, symbol taps) record a right as soon as they are done. Turn stages record nothing.</p>

<h3>5.2 Adaptive path (<code>operators.ts</code> over <code>lib/adaptiveFlow.ts</code>)</h3>
<p>Two ideas, both decided when the learner presses Continue on the last stage of a screen: a strong learner <b>skips</b> a repeat screen, and a learner who struggled is sent <b>back once</b> to the screen that taught the idea, then carries on from where they were.</p>
<h4>Skips</h4>
<p>A screen is skipped when <b>every</b> screen in its list was answered clean. A skipped screen itself counts as clean for the screens after it (the check is recursive: <code>cleanRight(s) = answeredClean(s) || SKIPS[s].every(cleanRight)</code>), so a chain of skips stays consistent.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Screen skipped</th><th>When all of these were clean</th></tr>
<tr><td>20 True or False?</td><td>19 Who is older?</td></tr>
<tr><td>23 Read what the expression says</td><td>22 Always read left to right</td></tr>
<tr><td>24 Write what the sentence says</td><td>22 and 23</td></tr>
<tr><td>30 Read, understand and evaluate</td><td>23 and 29</td></tr>
<tr><td>31 Choose the right operation</td><td>18 Understand the situation, and 30</td></tr>
</table>
<h4>Detours</h4>
<p>If the learner <b>struggled</b> on the screen they are leaving (<code>struggled(step)</code>), the screen has a detour and it has not been used before on this run, they go back to the detour screen; when they finish that screen (any Continue off its last stage) they resume at the screen that would have come after the one they struggled on (with skips applied).</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Struggled on</th><th>Sent back to</th></tr>
<tr><td>10 When sharing is not equal</td><td>9 Can everyone get the same number?</td></tr>
<tr><td>12 Not enough for even one group?</td><td>11 Same numbers, three answers!</td></tr>
<tr><td>13 How much remains?</td><td>12 Not enough for even one group?</td></tr>
<tr><td>16 Does calculation order matter?</td><td>15 Can you calculate the total bill?</td></tr>
<tr><td>17 Arrange the calculation</td><td>16 Does calculation order matter?</td></tr>
<tr><td>18 Understand the situation</td><td>15 Can you calculate the total bill?</td></tr>
<tr><td>20 True or False?</td><td>19 Who is older?</td></tr>
<tr><td>23 Read what the expression says</td><td>22 Always read left to right</td></tr>
<tr><td>24 Write what the sentence says</td><td>22 Always read left to right</td></tr>
<tr><td>27 At least 100 points</td><td>25 Six ways to compare values</td></tr>
<tr><td>28 Understanding the maximum limit</td><td>25 Six ways to compare values</td></tr>
<tr><td>30 Read, understand and evaluate</td><td>29 A comparison produces an answer</td></tr>
<tr><td>35 The incorrect shopping bill</td><td>15 Can you calculate the total bill?</td></tr>
<tr><td>36 The boundary bug</td><td>27 At least 100 points</td></tr>
<tr><td>37 Plan a class trip</td><td>12 Not enough for even one group?</td></tr>
<tr><td>38 Why did you choose these operations?</td><td>A function of how it went: if stage 0, 1 or 2 was missed (floor division, modulus, the extra bus) then screen 12; else if stage 4 or 5 was missed (the &lt;= questions) then screen 26 The number line challenge; else none.</td></tr>
</table>
<h4>The algorithm</h4>
<pre>skipped(s)   = SKIPS[s] exists and every screen in SKIPS[s] is cleanRight
plainNext(s) = first n &gt; s with n &lt;= 40 and not skipped(n); 41 means "complete"
href(n)      = n &gt; 40 ? /module/operators?step=complete : /module/operators?step=n

next(step):
  pending = read(detour key)                       // {to, resume} or null
  if pending and step == pending.to:               // finishing a detour screen
      clear pending; return href(pending.resume)
  after = plainNext(step)
  back  = DETOURS[step]  (a number, or a function returning a number or none)
  visited = read(detoured key) or {}
  if back is set and struggled(step) and not visited[step]:
      visited[step] = true; write(detoured key, visited)
      write(detour key, {to: back, resume: after})
      return /module/operators?step=back
  return href(after)

previous(step):
  if step &lt;= 1 return "/level"
  b = step - 1; while b &gt; 1 and skipped(b): b -= 1
  return step b
reset(): remove both keys</pre>
<ul>
  <li>Each screen can send the learner back only once per run (<code>visited</code>). On the detour screen itself no new detour starts (the pending check comes first).</li>
  <li>Keys: <code>lingo.operators-detour</code> (the pending <code>{ to, resume }</code>) and <code>lingo.operators-detoured</code> (the screens already sent back), both session storage. If storage is blocked nobody is sent back and nothing is skipped.</li>
  <li>The Back arrow does not clear a pending detour; the progress bar shows the screen number, so it moves backwards on a detour and forwards again after.</li>
</ul>

<h3>5.3 Streak strikes (progress bar and battery)</h3>
<p>The run of right answers in a row (<code>moduleStreak</code>) is shared by all modules and kept in session storage (<code>lingo.module-streak</code> = <code>{ streak, loops }</code>). Only a recorded answer moves it: every right quiz check, every <code>done()</code>, and every <code>slip()</code> (a wrong one). Opening or leaving a screen never does.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Right answers in a row</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
<tr><td>2nd</td><td><code>strikeDuo2</code> lightning on the bar (0.9 s)</td></tr>
<tr><td>3rd, 6th, 9th ...</td><td>Battery gains 1 unit (<code>BATTERY_UNITS = 1</code>) and plays its charge animation</td></tr>
<tr><td>4th</td><td><code>strikeMix</code> (1.6 s)</td></tr>
<tr><td>5th</td><td><code>strikeLoop</code> switch turns on and loops 1 s the first time, 4 s the second, 7 s afterwards</td></tr>
<tr><td>A wrong answer or <code>slip()</code></td><td>The run goes back to 0 and any strike is cancelled</td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
  <li>Each strike waits 150 ms after the correct / wrong trigger (two triggers in one frame would drop one). The Continue button plays the electric effect and takes no clicks until the strike is over; leaving the screen ends any loop. These screens have no hint button, so the plain strike clips play (not the <code>...Check</code> twins).</li>
</ul>

<h3>5.4 Battery</h3>
<ul>
  <li>The header battery (<code>battery.riv</code>) is the session's one battery. The learner starts at 18 of 20 units. <b>Arriving at a screen that has not been paid for spends 1 unit</b> (<code>chargeBatteryScreen(step)</code>): it pays for every screen from the furthest one paid so far up to this one, so jumping from 9 to 11 spends 2 units; Back and forward over paid screens spend nothing. Reaching screen 1 while the furthest paid screen is not 1 starts the module over (everything payable again; the level carries over). With the unlimited trial on, nothing is spent.</li>
  <li>The battery is charged by the streak (every 3rd right answer in a row, +1 unit) and refilled by the out-of-energy sheet. At 0 the stage is inert and the out-of-energy sheet appears (2.5).</li>
  <li>Skipped screens are never arrived at, so they cost nothing; a 40-screen module still costs more than the 18 units a learner starts with unless streaks or the trial refill it (see section 12).</li>
</ul>

<h3>5.5 Sounds and haptics</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>What</th><th>Details</th></tr>
<tr><td>Click sound</td><td><code>/audios/click-sound.mpeg</code>, preloaded; each tap plays a clone so quick taps overlap. Plays on every pick, tap, block, key, card and button press. Follows the header mute toggle. This is the only sound in the module.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tap haptic (110 ms)</td><td>Every pick and interaction tap, button press.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Correct answer haptic (250 ms)</td><td>Every right quiz check and every interaction <code>done()</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wrong answer haptic (80, 40, 80 ms)</td><td>Every wrong quiz check and every <code>slip()</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lesson complete (1200 ms), claim reward (350, 70, 350, 70, 600 ms)</td><td>On the Lesson complete screen and its reward chest.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>5.6 Saved state (all session storage unless noted)</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Key</th><th>Holds</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>lingo.operators-first-try</code></td><td>question id to right-first-time (boolean)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>lingo.operators-assisted</code></td><td>screen number to "miss" or "hint"</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>lingo.operators-rewards</code></td><td>reward ids already claimed (unused)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>lingo.operators-detour</code>, <code>lingo.operators-detoured</code></td><td>the pending detour <code>{ to, resume }</code>; screens already sent back</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>lingo.module-streak</code></td><td><code>{ streak, loops }</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>lingo.battery</code></td><td><code>{ level 0-100, unlimited, charged }</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>lingo:modules-done</code> (local storage), <code>lingo:module-just-done</code> (session)</td><td>How many modules are finished (the path), and a one-time flag so the path plays Hex moving on. <code>completeModule(5)</code> writes them (8).</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ===================================================== 6 -->
<h2>6. Interaction stages and scenes, one by one</h2>
<p>Each interaction below is a widget that draws itself in the room under Hex's line and talks to the screen through the stage <code>api</code> (<code>slip</code>, <code>done</code>, <code>finished</code>, <code>lineTyped</code>, <code>holdButton</code>, <code>next</code>). The screens in section 7 only list their data.</p>

<h3>6.1 ExpressionStage: build an expression from blocks</h3>
<p>Used on screens 4, 15, 17, 24, 26, 32, 35. Data: <code>lead</code> (a scene above), <code>prompt</code> (default "Tap the blocks to build it."), <code>tokens</code> (the blocks, in the order shown, each usable once), <code>accept</code> (every right way to write it, all the same length), <code>hint</code> (the wrong-try note), <code>result</code> (what it works out to).</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Part</th><th>Details</th></tr>
<tr><td>Layout</td><td>A 340 px column, 10 px gaps: the lead, the expression card, the tray of blocks, the note line.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Expression card</td><td>CARD look, white (dark #15181E), min height 68, full width, padding 12 x 8, blocks centred and wrapping, 6 px apart. Turns right-green when right, wrong-red while shaking.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Places</td><td>One place for each token of the accepted expressions (<code>length = accept[0].length</code>). Up to 5 places: "roomy" (place 44 x 48, placed block min 44 x 48, 21 px); more: "tight" (place 36 x 40, block min 36 x 40, 17 px; tray blocks 44, 19 px). An empty place is a dashed rounded (10 px) box, 2 px border: the next place to fill is picked out (teal border #08A4AD, fill teal 10%, gentle pulse); a place that only operators can fill (all accepted ways agree) has a teal 40% border; others #C9CED6 (dark white 20%).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Result slot</td><td>If <code>result</code> is given, after the places: a teal "=" (22 px bold #08A4AD) and a "?" pill (fill #E3F6F3, text #08A4AD, padding 12 x 6, 20 px bold). When right the "?" is replaced by the result in a #04908F pill with white text, springing in from scale 0.5 (stiffness 260, damping 14).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tray</td><td>Wrapping, centred, 8 px apart. Value blocks and operator blocks look as in 4.7 (tray blocks: roomy min 48 x 48, 21 px; padding 10). A used block fades to 25% opacity, drops 3 px and is disabled; press scale 0.92 and y 2.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tap a tray block</td><td>Ignored if right, wrong, finished, already used, or the places are full. Otherwise click sound + tap haptic; the block goes into the next free place with a spring (scale 0.6 to 1, opacity 0 to 1, stiffness 420, damping 22). When the last place is filled it is checked.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tap a placed block</td><td>Takes it back out (click sound only); the blocks after it close up to the left. Ignored while right, wrong or finished.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Check</td><td>The written tokens, in place order, are compared with every accepted way (same length, every token equal). Right: green look, the result appears, and 600 ms later <code>done()</code>. Wrong: <code>slip()</code>, the card shakes (x 0, -7, 7, -5, 5, 0 over 0.4 s) in the wrong look and the note shows the <code>hint</code>; after <b>800 ms</b> (<code>WRONG_MS</code>) every block goes back to the tray and the learner starts again.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Note line</td><td>13 px / 20 px, centred, min height 20: the <code>hint</code> in red #D42421 while the card is wrong; otherwise the prompt in grey #6B7480 (dark white 60%); empty once finished.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accepted ways</td><td><code>sumOfProducts([a,b],[c,d])</code> returns all 8 ways to write <code>a*b + c*d</code>: each product either way round, and the two products either way round (e.g. for 3,20,2,10: <code>3*20+2*10</code>, <code>20*3+2*10</code>, <code>3*20+10*2</code>, <code>20*3+10*2</code>, and the same four with the products swapped).</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>6.2 ShareStage: share equally, one round at a time</h3>
<p>Screens 9 (20 shared by 4) and 10 (23 shared by 4). A <code>ShareScene</code> (heap + trays, 6.10) over a pill button "Give one to each student" (fill #08A4AD, white 16 px bold, padding 24 x 8, fully round, edge <code>0 3px 0 #067F86</code>, presses 1 px down). Each tap: click sound, tap haptic, one more round: every student's tray gains one and the heap loses <code>students</code>. When fewer than <code>students</code> remain the button goes and 900 ms later <code>done()</code> is called; two chips fade in (8 px rise): "Each student has {rounds}" (fill #E3F6F3, text #04908F, 14 px bold) and "Left over: {pile}" (fill #FFF4E3, text #B85A28). 20 / 4 ends after 5 taps with 0 left; 23 / 4 after 5 taps with 3 left. No wrong path.</p>

<h3>6.3 FillRowsStage: put chairs in rows</h3>
<p>Screen 8 (4 rows of 5). A CREAM card (padding 8) of rows 44 px tall, 6 px apart. A row is a button: open = dashed 2 px #C9CED6 border on white 70%; full = solid #7FD8CC border on #E3F6F3. A row has a "Row n" chip (48 px wide, 11 px bold; open #EEF0F4 / #6B7480, full #04ACAB / white), <code>per</code> seat slots (26 px, 6 px radius, dotted 2 px border) and, at the end, a 24 px teal circle with the count that appears after <code>per * 0.16</code> s. Tapping an open row (click sound, tap haptic) fills it: its chairs pop in one after another (each 0.16 s after the one before: scale 0.3 to 1, y -8 to 0, opacity 0 to 1). Chair drawing: an SVG 26 px, back #04ACAB, seat #08A4AD, legs #444444 (unlit: #7C6BE0 / #5B3FD0). A filled row cannot be tapped again. Caption under the card (13 px, grey): "Tap a row to put its chairs in." then "n of 4 rows filled."; empty once finished. When the last row is full <code>done()</code> is called after <code>per * 160 + 700</code> ms. No wrong path.</p>

<h3>6.4 SymbolStage: big keys to tap, with an explanation card</h3>
<p>Screens 14, 21, 25, 27, 28, 29, 34, 39. Data: <code>groups[]</code> (each: optional <code>title</code>, <code>columns</code>, <code>items[]</code>), <code>need</code> (how many different keys must be tapped; default all), <code>header(current)</code> (a scene above that follows the last key tapped), <code>big</code> (default true), <code>prompt</code> (default "Tap each symbol.").</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Part</th><th>Details</th></tr>
<tr><td>Item</td><td><code>{ id, symbol, title, example?, say?, result? }</code>: the key's text, what it means, an example, the example read aloud, and whether the example is True (comparisons).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Layout</td><td>A 340 px column, 10 px gaps: the header, each group (optional heading, then a grid of keys <code>columns</code> wide, 10 px apart), then the explanation card, then <code>footer</code> once done (none used).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Group heading</td><td>12 px bold uppercase, letter-spacing 0.4, with a 6 px dot; in a screen with two groups the heading takes the group's colour, otherwise grey #6B7480.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Key</td><td>12 px radius, bold white text. Size: a symbol of more than 6 letters is 56 px tall at 17 px; otherwise <code>big</code>: 56 px tall at 28 px; not big: 44 px tall at 20 px. A symbol of 4 letters or fewer is set in a monospace font. Colours by group (first teal <code>#08A4AD</code> edge <code>#067F86</code>; second violet <code>#7B61FF</code> edge <code>#5B3FD0</code>; third orange <code>#E8743B</code> edge <code>#B85A28</code>); the key has a 3 px bottom edge. The tapped (current) key turns amber <code>#FFB300</code> with edge <code>#C98A00</code> and a soft ring (0 0 0 4px, 28% amber) and bounces (scale 1, 0.92, 1.08, 1 in 0.35 s). A key seen before (not current) carries a small tick badge (20 px circle #01A17F with a white 2 px ring, top right). Press: scale 0.94, y 2.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tap</td><td>Click sound + tap haptic; that key becomes current and is added to the seen list. When the number of different keys seen reaches <code>need</code> (the first time it does), <code>done()</code> is called 900 ms later. <b>Keys stay tappable after it is done</b> so the learner can keep exploring.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Explanation card</td><td>Min height 78, full width; the content cross-fades in when the current key changes (opacity 0 to 1, rise 6 px, 0.2 s). Empty state: the prompt (14 px / 20, grey). With a current key: white card (dark #15181E), shadow <code>0 2px 8px rgba(31,42,68,.12)</code>, 2 px border (transparent; #01A17F 40% if the example is True; #D42421 40% if False), radius 12, centred: the key's title (15 px bold #2F5D59, dark #BFE6E2); the example (monospace 18 px bold, with a pill "&#10003; True" #01A17F or "&#10005; False" #D42421, white 13 px bold, when <code>result</code> is set); the <code>say</code> sentence in quotes (13 px, italic, #6B7480).</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>6.5 StepStage: an expression worked out one step at a time</h3>
<p>Screens 16 (two stages) and 22 (two stages). A 340 px column: a white CARD (padding 12) with the big expression (<code>ExprBig</code>, with the current step's parts picked out and the result pill once given), a row of step dots, and a text box; under it a pill button. Dots: 6 px high, 6 px wide when still ahead (#D5DAE1, dark white 20%), 20 px wide and teal #08A4AD once reached (animates 0.3 s). Text box (min height 44, radius 10, 15 px / 22 px semibold, centred): idle "Tap the button to see the steps." (grey); a step's text in #2F5D59 on #EAF7F6 (dark #BFE6E2 on #0F2B28). The button reads "Show the steps" then "Next step" (the pill look of 6.2); each press: click sound, tap haptic, step +1. At the <b>last step</b> the button disappears and 1100 ms later <code>done()</code> is called. Each step is <code>{ lit: indexes of the tokens picked out, text, result? }</code>. No wrong path.</p>

<h3>6.6 OrderStage: put steps in order</h3>
<p>Screen 33. A 340 px column, 10 px gaps: a lead scene, then the steps in a shuffled order (<code>shown</code> gives the position in the right order of each step in turn), each a CREAM CARD button at least 46 px tall, padding 12 x 6, left to right: a 26 px circle (empty: dashed 2 px #C9CED6 ring; placed: filled #01A17F with the number in white and edge <code>0 2px 0 #018066</code>, springing from scale 0.5), then the step's text (14 px / 20, weight 600). Tap the steps in the order they happen: tapping the <b>next</b> step in the right order places it (green look, numbered, stays) with click sound + tap haptic; any other step: <code>slip()</code>, the row shakes (x 0, -7, 7, -5, 5, 0 over 0.4 s) in the wrong look for 800 ms and the note shows the hint. A placed step cannot be tapped again. When all are placed, 600 ms later <code>done()</code>. Note line (13 px): the hint in red; otherwise "Tap the steps in the order they happen." in grey; empty once finished.</p>

<h3>6.7 MultiPickStage (shared with Data Types): tap the cards that fit</h3>
<p>Screens 27 (stage 1) and 28 (stage 1). From <code>components/datatypes/interactions.tsx</code>. A 354 px column, 12 px gaps: a grid (3 columns here, 14 px gaps) of cards, then a note line. Card: CARD_BODY (8 px radius, 4 px border, shadow as 4.7), at least 64 px tall, padding 8, picture (none here) / label (16 px / 110%, weight 600, #444444) / sub label (12 px / 16, weight 500, #6B7480), tinted in turn (blue #E5EFFD, lavender #F6F4FE, green #ECF7ED, cream #FFF3DE; dark variants). Tap: click sound + tap haptic. A <b>right</b> card turns right-green (border #01A17F, fill #F2FBF8) and stays, with a scale pulse [1, 1.05, 1] in 0.35 s. A <b>wrong</b> card: <code>slip()</code>, shakes red (x 0, -7, 7, -5, 5, 0) for 600 ms and the note shows the hint (13 px, red #D42421). When every right card has been tapped, 500 ms later <code>done()</code>. No prompt line; the note is empty until a wrong tap.</p>

<h3>6.8 FlipCarouselStage: the flip-card carousel (screen 1)</h3>
<p>Four photo cards in a looping carousel; the centred card has its neighbours peeking in. Full stage width, height 386 (10 + 336 + 40), tinted band #04ACAB at 12% (dark 30%) fading to nothing at the top and bottom (a vertical mask: transparent, black 30%, black 70%, transparent). The web version also answers the left and right arrow keys.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Part</th><th>Details</th></tr>
<tr><td>Card</td><td>230 x 336 (the pictures are 1 : 1.459), centred horizontally, 10 px from the top. Front: the whole card is one picture (the scene, its name, rounded corners 26 px and a 4 px white edge are in the image). Back: the same shape in the card's tint (a vertical gradient), 4 px white border, content centred: "<b>{Verb} {Label}</b>" in 13 px bold uppercase teal #08A4AD with letter spacing, then the caption in 20 px / 28 semibold #2F5D59 (dark #BFE6E2). Tints: teal #E3F7F4 to #BDEBE5, pink #FDEBEF to #F8CFDA, blue #E6F0FF to #C9DEFF, amber #FFF3DC to #FFE1AB (dark variants exist).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Slots</td><td>The active card is at x = 0, scale 1, on top. The right neighbour is at +218 px, the left at -218 px, both scale 0.9; any other card is hidden (opacity 0) beyond the left one. The slide takes 0.7 s on <code>cubic-bezier(0.32, 0.72, 0, 1)</code>; opacity 0.25 s. A card that wraps round from one end to the other jumps instantly instead of sliding across.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Active highlight</td><td>The active card's picture gets a 2 px teal #08A4AD outline with a soft teal glow (<code>0 8px 14px rgba(8,164,173,.35)</code>); idle cards a soft grey drop shadow. A "Tap for more details" pill sits over the foot of the active card (78 px above its bottom) until the card is turned over: white 90%, teal 12 px bold text with a small hand icon, gentle pulse (scale 1 to 1.07, 1.6 s loop; none with reduced motion).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flip</td><td>Tapping the active card turns it over about its vertical axis (rotateY 0 to 180, 0.5 s, <code>cubic-bezier(0.45, 0, 0.25, 1)</code>, perspective 900); tapping again turns it back. A card that has been turned over carries a green tick badge (24 px circle #01A17F, top right). Tapping a neighbour moves it to the centre. Moving to another card turns any turned card face up.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dots</td><td>Four dots under the cards (bottom 12): the active one is a 24 x 8 pill with a light teal track (teal 25%) and a teal #08A4AD fill; others 8 x 8 grey #BFC3C9. Tap a dot to go to that card. While the autoplay runs the active dot's fill grows across the track over 2.6 s (linear) and restarts on every card; otherwise the active dot is simply full.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Swipe</td><td>Drag horizontally (elastic 0.2, snaps back): an offset beyond 50 px or a velocity beyond 400 moves one card (left swipe = next, right swipe = previous).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Demonstration</td><td>As soon as Hex has finished his line (<code>lineTyped</code>) plus 700 ms, the <b>first card turns over by itself</b>, holds its back up for 2400 ms and turns face up again (this counts as the first card seen). Then the carousel plays through the cards <b>once</b> on its own: each card stays 2600 ms, then slides to the next, ending back on the first. The first touch of any kind (tap, drag, key, dot) ends the demonstration and the autoplay for good. Reduced-motion users get neither.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Done</td><td>Once every card has been turned over (the demonstration counts for the first), the last card's back stays up 1600 ms and then <code>done()</code> is called (finish panel, confetti). No wrong path.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>6.9 Turn stages: SituationsStage, OperatorTurnStage, JobTurnStage</h3>
<p>Look-only stages where Hex introduces <b>one card at a time</b> (screens 2, 5, 6, 11). Each stage is "the turn" of card number <code>focus</code>; the screen puts one turn stage per card, so the next Continue shows the next card's turn (or a question). They never call <code>done()</code> and record nothing.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Part</th><th>Details</th></tr>
<tr><td>Timing (<code>useTurn</code>)</td><td>The stage opens; 500 ms later (<code>HIGHLIGHT_MS</code>) the focused card is "lit" (picked out). Once the card is lit <b>and</b> Hex has finished typing his line, the stage takes the main button (<code>holdButton</code>): Continue becomes enabled, and pressing it calls <code>next()</code> (go to the next stage, nothing recorded). Until then Continue is disabled.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Grid</td><td>A 340 px column. Situations: 2 columns, 12 px gaps, cards 150 px tall (icon 100 x 76 above the name in two lines: verb over thing, 14 px / 18 bold #444444). Operators and jobs (<code>TurnCardsStage</code>): 2 columns (jobs 150 px tall, operators 118 px) or 3 columns for the division screen (128 px tall, 10 px gaps), 12 px gaps, card art over the name (14 px / 18 bold); in a row of three, names reserve two lines so the keys line up.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Card look</td><td>10 px radius, 4 px border (white at rest; the lit card teal #08A4AD with a ring <code>0 0 0 3px rgba(8,164,173,.18)</code> and glow <code>0 6px 16px rgba(8,164,173,.25)</code>), idle shadow <code>1px 1px 6px 1px rgba(0,0,0,.15)</code>. Tints: lavender #F4F1FF, cream #FFF6EA, mint #EBF6F0, blue #E6F0FF (dark #1F1B3A, #3A2C14, #0F2B28, #15213A).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Highlight</td><td>The lit card scales to 1.04 on a spring (stiffness 320, damping 22) and (operators and jobs) its art hops up 5 px and back (0.45 s). In the operator and job turns the cards <b>not</b> being introduced fade to 30% opacity once the turn starts (and stay faded on the later turns, except the card whose turn just ended, which starts bright and hands over). Cards before the focus carry a green tick badge (24 px circle #01A17F, top 6 right 6).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Entrance</td><td>On the first turn (<code>focus 0</code>) the cards come in one after another (opacity 0 to 1, rise 16 px, scale 0.96 to 1, 0.35 s, 80 ms apart). Later turns find the cards already there (no entrance), so the change reads as a hand-over.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Example strip (operators, jobs)</td><td>Under the grid, a 60 px band with a white card (dark #15181E, radius 12, shadow <code>0 2px 8px rgba(31,42,68,.12)</code>, padding 12 x 8) that fades in (and rises 8 px, 0.3 s) once the card is lit: the card's chip (a small symbol key, or the job's picture in a 36 px circle #F4F6F8), the card's one-line meaning (13 px bold #2F5D59) and its example (monospace 20 px bold #444444).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Symbol key (SymbolTile)</td><td>The operator's symbol in white monospace bold on a rounded tile (large 56 px, radius 14, 34 px text; small 36 px, radius 10, 22 px; an asterisk is pushed down 8 px so it centres), edge <code>0 3px 0 rgba(0,0,0,.22)</code>, in the card's accent: lavender #7B61FF, cream #E8743B, mint #08A4AD, blue #3B82F6.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Header picture (division turns)</td><td>Screen 11's three turns put the <code>GroupsPicture</code> of 23 chocolates in groups of 4 over the cards (6.10), emphasising all, the complete groups, then the leftovers; before the card is lit the picture still shows the turn before so the change reads as a fade.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>6.10 Scenes (visuals), one by one</h3>
<p>Drawn in code in <code>visuals.tsx</code>; sizes in frame px. All come in with a small stagger and replay when a quiz is tried again (they receive <code>attempt</code> as <code>replay</code>).</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Scene</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>ValueCards</td><td>A row of named cards (default 316 wide, 6 px gaps), each a CARD (padding 6 x 8, LAVENDER by default; tone good or warn) with an optional 36 px white circle holding a 24 px picture, the name (11 px / 16 semibold uppercase, letter spacing 0.3, grey #6B7480) and the value (19 px / 24 bold #444444). Each card comes in 0.3 s after the one before (opacity, rise 12 px, scale 0.95 to 1, spring stiffness 260, damping 20). A sign badge (24 px teal circle, 12 px bold white, edge <code>0 2px 0 #067F86</code>) can stand between two cards.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Versus</td><td>ValueCards with two cards and the badge "vs" between them.</td></tr>
<tr><td>InfoSheet</td><td>"What we know": 316 px wide. A pill heading "What we know" (11 px bold uppercase teal #08A4AD on #E3F6F3, with a 16 px teal circle holding a white tick), a LAVENDER card of lines (picture in a 36 px white circle, label 14 px semibold grey, value 20 px bold #444444, hairline #E4DFF7 between lines; lines slide in from -12 px, 0.25 s apart), and under it, if there is a missing item, a dashed teal (60%) box with the item's greyed picture, its label and a pulsing "?" (22 px bold teal, scale 1 to 1.2, 1.6 s loop; none with reduced motion). Once its value is known the box turns solid teal-bordered on #E3F6F3 with the value (20 px bold #04908F, springing in). <code>compact</code>: no heading, smaller lines (32 px icon, 13 px label, 18 px value), 6 px gaps.</td></tr>
<tr><td>WalletScene</td><td>A CREAM card, 316 wide: a 56 px white circle with the wallet picture (40 px), the label "Wallet" (11 px grey uppercase) over the amount (26 px bold, "&#8377;50"). A pill slides in from the right (x +40, scale 0.8) after 0.6 s: "+ &#8377;30" in green #01A17F or "- &#8377;20" in orange #E8743B (16 px bold white). After 1300 ms the amount changes to the new total <b>only</b> if the learner has answered right (<code>showTotal = checked and correct</code>), so the scene never gives the answer away.</td></tr>
<tr><td>ExprBig</td><td>A big expression: tokens in a row (wrapping, 8 px apart; <code>tight</code> = no wrap, 2 px), bold <code>size</code> px (default 34; line-height 1.2), operators teal #08A4AD, parts "lit" on a yellow pad (#FFD36B, text #444444, edge <code>0 2px 0 #E0A81C</code>); each token has 6 px side padding and 8 px radius. A result renders as a pill "= {result}" (teal text #04908F on #E3F6F3, springing in from scale 0.7).</td></tr>
<tr><td>SideBySide</td><td>Two LAVENDER cards side by side (316 wide, 8 px gap, padding 12 x 8), each an ExprBig at 19 px, no wrap.</td></tr>
<tr><td>ShareScene</td><td>316 wide column, 8 px gaps: a CREAM card (min height 44, min width 150, padding 12 x 8) holding the heap (<code>Pile</code>, 10 chocolates a row) or the words "No chocolates left" (12 px bold grey); under it a row of student trays (6 px gaps).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pile and chocolate</td><td>A chocolate is a 13 px square (4 px radius), brown #8B5A3C with border #6B4226 and a light inset highlight; lit chocolates are yellow #FFD36B / border #FFB300. A pile is a grid of 13 px chocolates 3 px apart, <code>columns</code> wide (10 by default).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tray</td><td>A student's tray: flexible width, radius 10, 2 px border #B8A9F5 on #F1EEFF (dark #2E2A5C); a 28 px white circle with the student picture (20 px), the label "Student n" (11 px bold #5B3FD0), its chocolates (a pile 5 wide, min height 28), and the count (16 px bold #444444). A lit tray is amber (#FFB300 border, #FFF8E1 fill).</td></tr>
<tr><td>GroupsPicture</td><td>Chocolates set out in groups of <code>size</code> (316 wide, wrapping, 8 px gaps, centred): each complete group in a solid box (radius 10, 2 px #04ACAB border, fill #E3F6F3, padding 6, a pile at most 6 wide), then any leftovers in a dashed box (2 px dashed #FFB300, fill #FFF8E1). Boxes come in 0.25 s apart (opacity, scale 0.7 to 1); <code>still</code> = shown at once. <code>emphasis</code> = <code>groups</code> fades the leftovers box to 30%, <code>left</code> fades the complete groups to 30% (0.3 s).</td></tr>
<tr><td>PlantGrid</td><td>A CREAM card, padding 8, 4 x 4 cells of 32 px (4 px gaps), each with a plant picture (28 px). After 700 ms each row lights up in turn (a #FFD36B pad behind the plants), 450 ms apart; then each column in turn; then the highlight clears. Restarts on every try.</td></tr>
<tr><td>LineScale</td><td>A number line in a WHITE card (316 wide): a row of circles, one per mark (38 px, or 26 px when <code>small</code>; 15 or 11 px bold text), below them a 3 px grey #C9CED6 line with an arrow head. A circle can be "picked" (teal #08A4AD fill, #04ACAB border, white text) or "second" (orange #FFB267 fill, #E8743B border, white text); others white with a #C9CED6 border and grey text. With a <code>gate</code> the row is 66 px tall and a gate marker stands above that mark: its label (10 px bold #E8743B, e.g. "Unlock at 100", "Limit 150 cm") over a 3 x 22 px orange post.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CompareDemo</td><td>The computer's view of a comparison: two number blocks (LAVENDER CARD, min width 56, 26 px / 32 bold) either side of the symbol block (teal #08A4AD, white, edge <code>0 3px 0 #067F86</code>, same size), 8 px apart; the left block slides in from -20 px, the symbol pops (scale 0.4 to 1, delay 0.2 s), the right block slides in from +20 px; at 0.7 s a pill drops in under them (rise -8 px): "&#10003; True" (fill #01A17F, edge #018066) or "&#10005; False" (#D42421, edge #A31B19), white 16 px bold.</td></tr>
<tr><td>GateCard</td><td>A CARD (padding 16 x 8) with a lock icon (28 px; open #01A17F, locked #E8743B), the score (22 px bold) and "Unlocked" or "Locked" (16 px bold). Open: fill #E3F6F3 (dark #0F2B28); locked: #FFF4E3 (dark #4A3418).</td></tr>
<tr><td>TripFacts</td><td>Four small CARDs in a 2 x 2 grid (316 wide, 6 px gaps), each with a 36 px white circle holding a 24 px picture, a label (10 px bold uppercase grey) and a value (16 px bold): Students 32, Per bus 10, Cost per bus &#8377;2,000, Budget &#8377;7,000 (fills #F6F4FE, #E6F0FF, #FFF6EA, #EBF6F0).</td></tr>
<tr><td>BillCard</td><td>A CREAM card (316 wide, padding 12 x 8): lines "what ....... price" (14 px, a dotted #D9D2C4 leader between), then a dashed top rule and "Bill shown" (14 px bold) with a pill showing the total (17 px bold white; red #D42421 when <code>wrong</code>, else green #01A17F). The wrong bill on screen 35 has blank prices on its two lines.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flat icons</td><td>Notebook (blue book with white lines), pen (orange, rotated 45 degrees), bag (orange shopping bag): small SVGs drawn in code (32 x 32 viewBox) until the module has art. Flat icons for the chair (6.3) and the check tick are also drawn in code.</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ===================================================== 7 -->
<h2>7. Screens 1 to 40, one by one</h2>
<p>Every screen has a summary table and a stage table. Stages are numbered from 0 and come one after another on the same URL. How to read the stage table:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Kind:</b> a quiz (rows, a pair of tiles, or 2 x 2 tiles), an interaction (with the section where it is described), or a turn stage (6.9).</li>
  <li><b>Hex says:</b> the line in the bubble; it types in letter by letter (4.2).</li>
  <li><b>On screen:</b> the picture or interaction scene (section 6.10), the question pill if there is one, and what the option pictures are.</li>
  <li><b>Answers, keys or blocks:</b> the options in the order shown; <span class="ok">✔</span> marks the right one. The right answer is the same on a retry (the order never shuffles). For interactions it lists the data of the build (blocks, accepted ways, result, wrong-try note) or the keys.</li>
  <li><b>Result panel:</b> the right panel (title and text) and the wrong panel (title and text; the title is "Not quite" unless stated). A quiz shows one of them after Continue checks the answer. An interaction shows its finish panel when done, or none.</li>
  <li><b>Recorded:</b> which stage ids are written to the score (5.1).</li>
  <li>Money amounts use the rupee sign; expressions are written as in code (<code>*</code> multiply, <code>/</code> divide, <code>//</code> floor division, <code>%</code> remainder, <code>**</code> power).</li>
</ul>

