<tr><th>#</th><th>Kind</th><th>Hex says</th><th>On screen</th><th>Answers, keys or blocks (✔ = right)</th><th>Result panel</th></tr>
<tr><td>0</td><td>Interaction: SymbolStage</td><td>A game checks score &gt;= 100. Test two scores.</td><td>Header: before a tap, ExprBig "score &gt;= 100" at 30 px; after a tap, CompareDemo of the example with its result.<br>Two keys, 2 columns, not big (the symbol text is longer than 6 letters, so 56 px tall at 17 px). Prompt: "Tap each score."</td><td>Score = 120 | "Test the first score" | 120 &gt;= 100 | "One hundred twenty is greater than or equal to one hundred." | True<br>Score = 80 | "Test the second score" | 80 &gt;= 100 | "Eighty is greater than or equal to one hundred." | False<br>need = both</td><td>Finish panel "True or False!": A comparison doesn't return the bigger or smaller number. It returns a Boolean: True or False.</td></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Quiz, rows</td><td>What does a comparison like 80 &gt;= 100 give back?</td><td>No picture.<br>Option pictures: neutral icons.</td><td><span class="ok">✔ A Boolean: True or False.</span><br>The bigger number.<br>The smaller number.</td><td>Right panel "Exactly!": Boolean is the data type for True/False information. A comparison can be read, understood and evaluated.<br>Wrong panel "Not quite": Remember the Boolean data type from the last chapter.</td></tr>
</table>


