<table>
<tr><th>#</th><th>Kind</th><th>Hex says</th><th>On screen</th><th>Answers, keys or blocks (✔ = right)</th><th>Result panel</th></tr>
<tr><td>0</td><td>Quiz, rows</td><td>Why did we use floor division, 32 // 10?</td><td>No picture.<br>Option pictures: neutral icons.</td><td><span class="ok">✔ To find the complete groups of 10.</span><br>To get a decimal answer.<br>To find who is left over.</td><td>Right panel "Well explained!": // counts complete groups: 3 full buses.<br>Wrong panel "Not quite": Which operation counts complete groups?</td></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Quiz, rows</td><td>Why did we use modulus, 32 % 10?</td><td>No picture.<br>Option pictures: neutral icons.</td><td>To count the complete buses.<br><span class="ok">✔ To find how many students are left over.</span><br>To work out the cost.</td><td>Right panel "Well explained!": % gives the remainder: 2 students left.<br>Wrong panel "Not quite": Which operation gives what is left over?</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Quiz, rows</td><td>Why did we need an extra bus?</td><td>No picture.<br>Option pictures: neutral icons.</td><td>Floor division rounds up.<br>Buses cost more than ₹7,000.<br><span class="ok">✔ 2 students remain without a seat.</span></td><td>Right panel "Well explained!": Two students remain, so the remainder needs a bus too.<br>Wrong panel "Not quite": Think about the students who are left after 3 full buses.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Quiz, rows</td><td>Why did we use multiplication for the total cost?</td><td>No picture.<br>Option pictures: neutral icons.</td><td>We are sharing the cost.<br>We are comparing costs.<br><span class="ok">✔ Every bus costs the same, so the cost repeats.</span></td><td>Right panel "Well explained!": Equal items repeat: 4 buses * ₹2,000.<br>Wrong panel "Not quite": Four buses, each with the same cost: what repeats?</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Quiz, rows</td><td>Why did we use &lt;= to compare the cost with the budget?</td><td>No picture.<br>Option pictures: neutral icons.</td><td><span class="ok">✔ Spending exactly the budget is still allowed.</span><br>It finds the remainder.<br>It divides the cost.</td><td>Right panel "Well explained!": &lt;= includes the budget itself.<br>Wrong panel "Not quite": Which operator asks “Is it within the limit?”</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Quiz, rows</td><td>How would the answer change if the budget were ₹8,000?</td><td>No picture.<br>Option pictures: neutral icons.</td><td>It would stay False.<br><span class="ok">✔ 8000 &lt;= 8000 is True: the trip works.</span><br>We would need another bus.</td><td>Right panel "Well explained!": The cost equals the budget, and &lt;= allows that.<br>Wrong panel "Not quite": Is 8000 less than or equal to 8000?</td></tr>
</table>


<h3>Screen 39: You Can Now Make Values Work! (/module/operators?step=39)</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Item</th><th>Details</th></tr>
<tr><td>Headline</td><td>"You Can Now Make Values Work!" (teal: "Make Values Work!"), two lines at 24 px</td></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Your operator toolkit: tap the arithmetic and comparison operators; five of the thirteen finish the stage, all stay tappable.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stages</td><td>1 (Tap the toolkit)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recorded</td><td>Quiz stages and finished interactions record: ids 390.</td></tr>
</table>
<table>
<tr><th>#</th><th>Kind</th><th>Hex says</th><th>On screen</th><th>Answers, keys or blocks (✔ = right)</th><th>Result panel</th></tr>
<tr><td>0</td><td>Interaction: SymbolStage</td><td>Tap an operator: its meaning, how it reads and an example.</td><td>Two groups, not big (44 px keys, 20 px text): the heading "ARITHMETIC OPERATORS" in teal over 4 columns (7 keys, two rows), then "COMPARISON OPERATORS" in violet over 3 columns (6 keys). No header scene. Prompt: "Tap each symbol."</td><td>Arithmetic keys (symbol | meaning | example | read aloud):<br>+ | Add values | 3 + 2 = 5 | "Three plus two is five."<br>- | Subtract values | 5 - 2 = 3 | "Five minus two is three."<br>* | Multiply values | 4 * 5 = 20 | "Four times five is twenty."<br>/ | Ordinary division | 20 / 4 = 5.0 | "Twenty divided by four is five point zero."<br>// | Floor division | 23 // 4 = 5 | "Complete groups of four in twenty-three: five."<br>% | Find the remainder | 23 % 4 = 3 | "Twenty-three leaves a remainder of three."<br>** | Raise to a power | 4 ** 2 = 16 | "Four to the power of two is sixteen."<br>Comparison keys (all True):<br>== | Equal to | 10 == 10 | "Ten is equal to ten." | True<br>!= | Not equal to | 10 != 5 | "Ten is not equal to five." | True<br>&gt; | Left value is greater | 10 &gt; 5 | "Ten is greater than five." | True<br>&lt; | Left value is smaller | 5 &lt; 10 | "Five is less than ten." | True<br>&gt;= | Greater than or equal to | 10 &gt;= 10 | "Ten is greater than or equal to ten." | True<br>&lt;= | Less than or equal to | 5 &lt;= 10 | "Five is less than or equal to ten." | True<br>need = 5 different keys</td><td>Finish panel "Your toolkit!": You can calculate and compare values. Keep tapping to explore the rest.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Screen 40: From Calculation to Decision (/module/operators?step=40)</h3>
<p>The only screen with its own layout (<code>RecapScreen</code>, not <code>OperatorsScreen</code>). It has no stages, no Hex line and no question.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Item</th><th>Details</th></tr>
<tr><td>Headline</td><td>"From Calculation <b>to Decision</b>" (teal: "to Decision"), 28 letters, two lines at 24 px, in the usual place.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Close the chapter: the application's flow from a calculation to a decision, what a program can now do, the final thought, and the lead into Conditions.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Layout</td><td>One column, 340 px wide, centred, from stage top 168, 8 px gaps. Nothing is scored; nothing is recorded.</td></tr>
<tr><td>1. Flow card</td><td>A LAVENDER CARD (padding 10). Row 1: three boxes joined by arrows: "Shopping total" (plain), arrow, "Compare with budget" (key: fill #08A4AD, white text, edge <code>0 3px 0 #067F86</code>), arrow, "True or False" (plain). Boxes: flexible width, min height 44, radius 10, padding 8 x 4, 13 px / 16 bold, centred. Each box fades in and drops from -8 px (0.35 s) at 0.2, 0.5 and 0.8 s; each arrow (16 px, teal, 3.5 px stroke) fades in sliding from -4 px (0.25 s) at 0.4 and 0.7 s.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2. Branch lines</td><td>A 20 px tall SVG across the card (viewBox 100 x 20, stretched; stroke teal 2.5 px, round caps, non-scaling): a vertical from the "True or False" box (at 85% of the width) down to y 10, a horizontal at y 10 from x 85 to x 24, then a drop at x 24 and a drop at x 76 to the bottom. It draws itself (path length 0 to 1, 0.5 s easeOut) from 1.05 s.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3. Two outcome boxes</td><td>Two boxes side by side (8 px gap, equal height, centred under x 24% and x 76%). True (good: fill #E3F6F3, text #04908F; dark #0F2B28 / #7FE0D6; appears at 1.5 s): a 16 px green circle with a tick and "True", then "Within budget → Continue purchase" (semibold). False (warn: fill #FFF4E3, text #A85B00; dark #4A3418 / #FFB267; appears at 1.65 s): a 16 px orange circle with "!" and "False", then "Over budget → Show a warning".</td></tr>
<tr><td>4. "A program can now"</td><td>A small heading (12 px bold uppercase, letter spacing 0.4, grey #6B7480), then a box (fill #E3F6F3, dark #0F2B28, radius 14, padding 14 x 10, 6 px gaps) of five lines, each sliding in from -10 px (0.3 s), the first at 1.9 s, 0.15 s apart: an 18 px teal #04ACAB circle with a white tick (edge <code>0 1px 0 #067F86</code>) and the text (13 px / 18 medium, #2F5D59, dark #BFE6E2): "Calculate a shopping bill." "Find how much money remains." "Divide objects into complete groups and find the remainder." "Compare a score with a target." "Check whether a purchase is within budget."</td></tr>
<tr><td>5. Final thought</td><td>A box (fill #F1EEFF, dark #23204A, radius 14, padding 14 x 10): a 16 px quote icon (#6D4FE0, dark #A79BFF) and the text (13 px / 18 semibold, #444444): "Operators help a program calculate and compare values. The result of a comparison can help a program make a decision."</td></tr>
<tr><td>6. Lead-in line</td><td>Centred, 12 px / 16 semibold grey: a pill "NEXT" (fill #08A4AD, white, 10 px bold uppercase, letter spacing 0.4, padding 8 x 2) and "Conditions: teaching a program to make decisions."</td></tr>
<tr><td>Button</td><td>"Complete Chapter" (enabled at once, no hint). Opens the gem popup (below). Back goes to <code>operatorsPrevious(40)</code> (screen 39).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gem popup (GemPopup)</td><td>Dark overlay (black 45%, 0.25 s fade). Card 356 x 494, 32 px radius, white (dark #1A1C22), springs in (scale 0.85 to 1, y 24 to 0, stiffness 260, damping 22), exits with scale 0.92. Content at fixed spots: close X (39 px square, top 24 right 24); "+10 XP" (32 px, #FF8D42, top 22); the animated gem (Rive file <code>gem</code>, artboard <code>Gem</code>, state machine <code>State Machine 1</code>, 156 x 156 at top 63); "Congratulations!" (32 px semibold, top 219); a mint chip (154 x 63.6, #E9F8F4) with the small gem (tilted -19.9 degrees) and "1 Gem" (24 px); a sparkle divider (top 368); "You won 1 Gem as you followed the given command." (14 px, 265 wide, top 410). A countdown bar along the bottom (6 px, teal) drains left to right over 3 s (linear); the popup closes itself at 3 s. Tap anywhere or the X to close early (click sound). Closing goes to <code>/module/operators?step=complete</code>.</td></tr>
</table>

