<!-- ===================================================== 11 -->
<h2>11. Building it in Flutter</h2>

<h3>11.1 From the web to Flutter</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>On the web</th><th>In Flutter</th></tr>
<tr><td>390 x 844 frame scaled as one piece</td><td>A <code>LayoutBuilder</code> that computes <code>scale = min(w/390, h/844, 1.5)</code>, then lays the stage out in frame units (<code>stageW = w/scale</code>, <code>stageH = h/scale</code>) inside a <code>Transform.scale</code> (or <code>FittedBox</code>) so every number in this document can be used as written. <code>colW = min(stageW - 50/scale, 380/scale)</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>stageTop(figmaTop)</code></td><td><code>figmaTop - 37</code> for anything pinned to the top.</td></tr>
<tr><td>framer-motion springs <code>{ stiffness, damping }</code></td><td><code>SpringDescription(mass: 1, stiffness: s, damping: d)</code> with a <code>SpringSimulation</code> (or <code>physics</code> package). Values used: title 140/16, cards in 110/13, panel 360/32, bubble 260/20, placed block 420/22, result pill 260/14, lit card 320/22, ValueCards 260/20, XP 300/9, gem popup 260/22, out of energy 320/34, sheet 360/32.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Keyframes <code>[1, 0.95, 1.05, 1]</code></td><td>A <code>TweenSequence</code> of equal segments (framer eases each with ease-in-out unless told otherwise). Shake <code>[0, -7, 7, -5, 5, 0]</code> likewise.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CSS cubic-bezier, easeOut, backIn</td><td><code>Cubic(a, b, c, d)</code> with the numbers in this document; <code>easeOut</code> = <code>Curves.easeOut</code>; the bubble's exit <code>backIn</code> = <code>Cubic(0.36, 0, 0.66, -0.56)</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typed bubble text</td><td>A timer: 350 ms, then one more letter every 55 ms. Lay the <b>whole</b> line out from the start with a <code>RichText</code> whose untyped part has a transparent colour, so centred text never shifts. Report "typed" when the last letter shows.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Teal gradient text</td><td><code>ShaderMask</code> with a <code>LinearGradient</code> from #00B8A9 to #1387B3 at stop 0.3317 (then solid).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bubble's drop edge (<code>drop-shadow 0 3px 0</code>)</td><td>A shape painted twice: the bubble outline path (box + tail) offset 3 px down in rgba(18,140,179,0.5), then the white shape on top (a <code>CustomPainter</code>).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Picture auto-fit (<code>ResizeObserver</code>)</td><td>Measure the room and the picture (<code>LayoutBuilder</code> and a <code>GlobalKey</code> size read after layout, or a custom render object) and scale by <code>clamp(room / picture, 0.6, 1)</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>pointer-events-none</code></td><td><code>IgnorePointer</code> for the overlay layers; the stage's interactive parts re-enable taps.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3D flip (rotateY, perspective 900)</td><td><code>Transform(transform: Matrix4.identity()..setEntry(3, 2, 1/900)..rotateY(angle))</code>; show the front for angles under 90 degrees and the back (mirrored by another 180) above.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Carousel drag</td><td><code>GestureDetector</code> horizontal drag with the 50 px / 400 px/s thresholds, elastic factor 0.2 while dragging, snap back.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Session storage</td><td>An in-memory store (cleared when the module starts) holding the keys in 5.6; use <code>shared_preferences</code> only where a value must survive an app restart (<code>lingo:modules-done</code>).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pages and <code>?step=</code></td><td>One <code>OperatorsModulePage</code> holding the current step (1 to 40 or complete); build the screen with <code>key: ValueKey(step)</code> so each starts fresh. Continue and Back set the step with no page transition (the web changes page).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reduced motion</td><td><code>MediaQuery.of(context).disableAnimations</code>: no flip demonstration or autoplay, no pulsing "?" or hint.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dark mode</td><td>Use the dark values in section 9; the soft gradient is light only.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rive</td><td>Hex, the progress bar, the Continue button, the battery and Lesson complete: the existing Flutter Rive layer (<code>flutter-rive-implementation.md</code>, <code>hex-riv-implementation.md</code> part 3). The robot and the flip-card Rive are not needed.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>11.2 A data model that fits every screen</h3>
<pre>sealed class Stage { String? line; }

class QuizStage extends Stage {
  String? question;                          // the pill above the answers
  Widget Function(VisualContext)? visual;    // VisualContext(attempt, picked, checked, correct)
  QuizLayout layout = QuizLayout.rows;       // rows | grid (2 = pair, 3 = tiles, 4 = 2x2)
  List&lt;Option&gt; options;                       // Option(id, text, iconAsset, tint?)
  String correctId, rightTitle, right, wrong;
  String wrongTitle = 'Not quite';
  bool keepLine = false;
}

class InteractionStage extends Stage {
  Widget Function(InteractionApi) render;
  ({String title, String text})? finishPanel;
}

class InteractionApi {            // what a stage can ask of the screen
  void Function() slip, done, next;
  void Function(VoidCallback?) holdButton;
  bool finished, lineTyped;
}

class ScreenDef { int step; String title; List&lt;Stage&gt; stages; }   // title: '*teal*' marks</pre>

<h3>11.3 The two pieces of pure logic</h3>
<p>These have no UI and are easy to unit-test. They are shown in Dart-like form; the web source is <code>moduleScore.ts</code> and <code>adaptiveFlow.ts</code>.</p>
<pre>// Score
int questionId(int step, [int stage = 0]) =&gt; step * 10 + stage;
void noteAnswer(int step, int stage, bool correct) {           // first answer only
  final id = questionId(step, stage);
  if (!firstTry.containsKey(id)) firstTry[id] = correct;
}
void noteStruggle(int step, Assist kind) {                     // miss outranks hint
  if (assisted[step] == Assist.miss) return;
  assisted[step] = kind;
}
bool struggled(int step) =&gt; assisted.containsKey(step);
bool missedStage(int step, int stage) =&gt; firstTry[questionId(step, stage)] == false;
bool answeredClean(int step) =&gt; firstTry[questionId(step)] == true &amp;&amp; !struggled(step);
int accuracy() =&gt; firstTry.isEmpty ? 100 : (100 * firstTry.values.where((v) =&gt; v).length / firstTry.length).round();

// Flow
bool cleanRight(int s) =&gt; answeredClean(s) || (skips[s]?.every(cleanRight) ?? false);
bool skipped(int s) =&gt; skips[s]?.every(cleanRight) ?? false;
int plainNext(int s) { var n = s + 1; while (n &lt;= 40 &amp;&amp; skipped(n)) n++; return n; }

Target next(int step) {
  final pending = store.detour;                                // {to, resume}?
  if (pending != null &amp;&amp; step == pending.to) { store.detour = null; return screen(pending.resume); }
  final after = plainNext(step);
  final back = detours[step]?.call();                          // int?; screen 38 is a function
  if (back != null &amp;&amp; struggled(step) &amp;&amp; !store.detoured.contains(step)) {
    store.detoured.add(step);
    store.detour = (to: back, resume: after);
    return screen(back);
  }
  return screen(after);                                        // 41 means complete
}
Target previous(int step) {
  if (step &lt;= 1) return levelPath;
  var b = step - 1; while (b &gt; 1 &amp;&amp; skipped(b)) b--;
  return screen(b);
}</pre>
<p><b>Worked examples to test against</b> (all with a fresh score):</p>
<ul>
  <li>Answer screen 19's two questions right first time, no misses: leaving 19 goes to <b>21</b> (20 is skipped). Back from 21 goes to 19.</li>
  <li>Miss one question on screen 22: screen 22 has no detour, so leaving it goes to 23 (23 is not skipped, because 22 is not clean).</li>
  <li>Miss once on screen 12 (any stage): Continue from 12 goes to <b>11</b> and stores <code>{ to: 11, resume: 13 }</code>. Continue from 11 goes to <b>13</b> and clears it. Missing on 12 again later (a replay) sends nobody back.</li>
  <li>Screen 38: miss stage 1 (the modulus question): Continue from 38 goes to <b>12</b>. Miss only stage 4: goes to <b>26</b>, and from 26 on to <b>39</b>. Miss stages 1 and 4: goes to 12.</li>
  <li>Screens 18 and 30 both clean (30 answered clean, or skipped because 23 and 29 were clean): screen 31 is skipped, so leaving 30 goes to 32.</li>
</ul>

<h3>11.4 Expression checking (screens 4, 15, 17, 24, 26, 32, 35)</h3>
<pre>bool isOperator(String t) =&gt; RegExp(r'^([+\-*/%&lt;&gt;=!()]+|and|or|not)$').hasMatch(t);

// a place takes: value | operator | any, from what every accepted way agrees on
SlotKind kindOf(int place) {
  final ops = accept.map((way) =&gt; isOperator(way[place])).toSet();
  return ops.length &gt; 1 ? SlotKind.any : (ops.contains(true) ? SlotKind.operator : SlotKind.value);
}

void onFilled(List&lt;int&gt; chosenTokenIndexes) {
  final written = chosenTokenIndexes.map((i) =&gt; tokens[i]).toList();
  final ok = accept.any((way) =&gt; way.length == written.length &amp;&amp; Iterable.generate(way.length).every((i) =&gt; way[i] == written[i]));
  if (ok) { showResult(); Future.delayed(600.ms, api.done); }
  else    { api.slip(); shake(); Future.delayed(800.ms, clearAll); }   // note shows the hint meanwhile
}

List&lt;List&lt;String&gt;&gt; sumOfProducts((String, String) a, (String, String) b) {
  List&lt;List&lt;String&gt;&gt; term((String, String) p) =&gt; [[p.$1, '*', p.$2], [p.$2, '*', p.$1]];
  return [ for (final x in term(a)) for (final y in term(b)) ...[[...x, '+', ...y], [...y, '+', ...x]] ];
}</pre>

<h3>11.5 Build order and checklist</h3>
<ol>
  <li><b>Shell and scale:</b> frame scaling, header, Continue row (labels and disabled rules of 2.4), SoftBackdrop, out-of-energy sheet.</li>
  <li><b>Quiz stage end to end</b> (this covers 82 of the 129 stages): headline, Hex bubble with typing, the picture room with auto-fit, answer rows, tiles (pair and 2 x 2), question pill, result panel, check / Try again / Next, confetti and XP. Check the geometry numbers of 3.3 on a 390 px and a wide device.</li>
  <li><b>Scoring and the flow</b> (11.3) with unit tests of the worked examples, then wire Back and Continue to them. Wire the progress bar reactions, streak and battery.</li>
  <li><b>Interactions</b> in this order (most screens first): Expression (7 screens), Symbol (8), Step (2), Share (2), Order, Fill rows, Multi-pick (2); then the scenes of 6.10 as the screens that use them are built.</li>
  <li><b>Turn stages</b> (screens 2, 5, 6, 11) and the <b>flip carousel</b> (screen 1).</li>
  <li><b>Screen 40, Lesson complete and the reward chest.</b></li>
  <li><b>Walk every screen:</b> all 40 in order with right answers; then with a wrong answer on each screen to see its note, shake and Try again; then the skip and detour paths of 5.2; Back from every screen; dark mode; a small and a wide device; reduced motion.</li>
</ol>

<!-- ===================================================== 12 -->
<h2>12. Known notes and gaps found in the code</h2>
<p>These are observations from reading the source, not changes.</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>No hint button anywhere.</b> The engine supports <code>hintText</code> (and a hint button with the orange fill), but no Operators stage sets it, so the bottom row is always the single Continue button and <code>noteStruggle(..., "hint")</code> never runs.</li>
  <li><b>Text length rules not applied here yet.</b> The one-line headline, one-line option and two-line bubble rules that were applied to the Data Types screens have not been applied to Operators: 63 of the 129 Hex lines are longer than about 46 letters (so they take three or more lines in the 248 px bubble), 44 of the 161 answer-row options are longer than 24 letters (they wrap to two lines inside the row), and 69 result-panel texts are longer than about 52 letters (more than two lines in the 190 px column). 20 of the 40 headlines run to two lines. Build the Flutter widgets to wrap and grow (do not truncate); if the copy is later shortened, only the data changes.</li>
  <li><b>Bubble width rule.</b> <code>bubbleSize</code> here puts every line of 50 letters or fewer in a 190 px bubble (about 17 letters a line). The Data Types screen uses a different rule (190 only up to 30 letters, otherwise about 248).</li>
  <li><b>Battery.</b> Every screen arrived at costs 1 of 20 units and a learner starts with 18, so 40 screens cannot be played from the start without streak charges (every 3rd right answer in a row), the unlimited trial, or a recharge; skipped screens are free, jumps pay for the screens they skip over.</li>
  <li><b>Look-only stages count as right answers.</b> Finishing the flip carousel, a step-by-step or a fill-the-rows stage calls <code>done()</code>, which records a right first try, plays the bar's <code>correct</code> reaction and raises the streak, although nothing was asked. They count towards the accuracy shown on Lesson complete (which therefore is not only about questions).</li>
  <li><b>Turn stages record nothing.</b> Screens 2, 5, 6 and 11 begin with turn stages, so their stage 0 never records. None of those four screens is in a skip list, so this changes nothing today; a screen whose stage 0 is a turn stage could never be "clean".</li>
  <li><b>The clean check looks at stage 0 only</b> (plus "no miss anywhere on the screen"). That is enough for the screens that are skipped on (19, 22, 23, 29, 18, 30) because any later miss marks the whole screen as struggled.</li>
  <li><b>Continue waits for Hex on the turn stages.</b> The button is disabled until the line is typed (350 ms + 55 ms a letter, so a 70-letter line takes about 4.2 s) and the card is lit (500 ms).</li>
  <li><b>Screen 9, stage 0</b> is a prediction that is still graded: the right answer is titled "Good guess!" and a wrong one says "Let's see by sharing them", but the learner must pick the right number to carry on (Try again), before the sharing is shown.</li>
  <li><b>Screen 16, step 3 of the first step-through</b> lights the "2" and the "+" (tokens 0 and 1) for "Then add 2: 2 + 12", and the result 14 appears; the 12 itself is not lit.</li>
  <li><b>Fixed values.</b> Lesson complete shows XP +40 and the time 3:42 (222 s) as fixed values; the "+10 XP" burst is decoration only (no total is kept).</li>
  <li><b>Unused code.</b> <code>ScenesStage</code> (tap each scene), the <code>after</code> props of ShareStage and FillRowsStage, <code>QuizStage.compact</code>, <code>P.game</code>, <code>P.gate</code> and several other stand-in pictures are not used by any screen. <code>RobotWarmup</code> preloads the robot file although this module never shows it.</li>
  <li><b>Pictures are stand-ins.</b> Except the screen 1 and 2 art (<code>operators-module/</code>), every picture on an option or card is borrowed from other modules (listed in section 10) and drawn flat icons stand in for the notebook, pen and bag. An idea keeps one picture wherever it appears (money is always the coins).</li>
  <li><b>Dark mode</b> classes exist on every component here and the module layout does not force a theme, but the dark look was not checked while writing this document; the soft background gradient is light only.</li>
  <li><b>Accessibility.</b> Only the flip carousel, the information sheet's "?" and the tap hint react to reduced motion; the other animations always play.</li>
</ul>

<p class="meta">End of document.</p>
